<h2 id="sec-1">The Verified Sirius Monthly Rate</h2>
<p>The verified figures for the Yamaha Sirius are 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week and 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND per month. The monthly rate is a range, and the honest way to treat a range is to plan against its top and be pleased by anything lower. Where inside the range a specific machine lands depends on condition and availability on the day, which the counter confirms.</p>
<p>What the range buys is a month of transport. Against the daily rate, the monthly range works out to the equivalent of six to eight rented days, which is why anyone staying three weeks or longer is usually already on the monthly side of the arithmetic. The full daily-to-monthly progression for every model is laid out in the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a>, where the Sirius can be compared line by line with the rest of the fleet.</p>
<p>One rule keeps this guide honest: everything here is a verified price or a cautious instruction to ask. Nothing is estimated, discounted or projected, because the only price that matters is the one you are quoted on the day, by the people who set it.</p>
<h2 id="sec-2">What the Monthly Range Means</h2>
<p>A range is not vagueness; it is honesty about variability. The Sirius fleet contains more than one machine, machines age differently, and rental terms differ in small ways that the counter settles case by case. Quoting a single number would be cleaner and less true, and this shop quotes what it can stand behind.</p>
<p>For budgeting, the practical reading of 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND is simple: set aside the upper figure, ask early which end applies to the machine you are looking at, and let the answer either confirm the plan or start a conversation about alternatives. The same logic applies to every model with a monthly range in the fleet.</p>
<p>The instruction that follows from all of this is short: confirm the exact figure before committing. A two-minute conversation at 112 Nguyen Van Cu fixes the number precisely, and no written guide, this one included, can honestly quote it in advance.</p>
<h2 id="sec-3">Comparing Monthly Rates Across the Fleet</h2>
<p>The Sirius shares its monthly range with the Honda Wave, Honda Click and Yamaha Mio, all verified at 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND per month, which makes these four the budget tier of a month-long rental. The Honda Air Blade sits at a flat 1,500,000 VND per month, and the Honda Vision at 1,800,000 to 2,000,000 VND per month, forming the higher tier.</p>
<p>The honest comparison is not about which number is lowest but about which machine earns its number for your month. A rider spending the month on short city trips has one answer; a rider planning longer rides has another. The daily and weekly rates follow the same tiering, 150,000 VND per day and 700,000 VND per week for the budget tier against 200,000 VND per day and 1,000,000 VND per week above it, so the relative cost holds across every rental period.</p>
<p>Reading the tiers side by side in the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a> is the fastest way to see the whole structure, and it keeps the comparison grounded in verified figures rather than memory or secondhand quotes.</p>
<h2 id="sec-4">Monthly Versus Daily and Weekly Math</h2>
<p>The arithmetic of switching points is straightforward. Daily at 150,000 VND, the weekly rate of 700,000 VND pays for itself in five days, and the monthly range pays for itself somewhere between six and eight days of equivalent daily rental. Anyone riding roughly every second day is already close; anyone riding daily is far past it.</p>
<p>The reverse check matters too. A rider who will use the machine only a handful of days spread across a month may be better served by daily or weekly rentals than a monthly commitment, and the honest budget weighs convenience against pure cost. Monthly wins the arithmetic in most riding-heavy cases; daily wins in flexibility cases.</p>
<p>Whichever side of the line a stay falls on, the numbers above are all verified, so the comparison can be finished on paper before it is finished at the counter, which is exactly how a confident decision is made.</p>
<h2 id="sec-5">Planning a Monthly Budget Around the Sirius</h2>
<p>A month on a Sirius has three cost lines: the rental, the fuel and the small incidentals of daily riding. The rental is the verified 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND, the fixed anchor of the budget. Fuel scales with riding, and the honest habit is a small daily allowance set slightly above expected use, with the surplus rolling forward.</p>
<p>The practical monthly budget, then, is the top of the rental range plus the fuel allowance plus a small margin, planned as one number rather than three. Riders who set the number in advance stop thinking about money mid-month and start thinking about riding, which is the point of the whole exercise.</p>
<p>For the wider planning questions that surround a month in Hanoi, from rental terms to what to check before committing, the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> collects the practical answers, and reading it before the counter conversation makes the conversation shorter.</p>
<h2 id="sec-6">Questions Worth Asking at the Counter</h2>
<p>The monthly conversation deserves its own short list. Ask where inside the range the available machines sit today. Ask what the rental terms cover for a month-long rental, since terms are confirmed at the counter rather than assumed from a website. Ask about the machine’s condition and service history in plain language, which any shop that rents the same models daily can answer easily.</p>
<p>Ask what to do if something needs attention during the month, because knowing the procedure in advance is worth more than any promise. And ask the question this guide keeps returning to: the exact figure for the exact machine, written on your paperwork, so there is nothing to reconcile later.</p>
<p>None of these questions is unreasonable, and at 112 Nguyen Van Cu the staff answer them daily. A renter who asks them arrives at a monthly decision that holds for thirty days instead of one that needs renegotiating in week two.</p>
<h2 id="sec-7">Who the Sirius Suits for a Month</h2>
<p>The Sirius is the fleet’s honest workhorse, and a month suits it. For riders whose month is mostly city riding, errands and a regular commute, it does the work without drama and without charging extra for style. Its monthly range sits in the budget tier, which keeps the fixed cost of a month low.</p>
<p>It equally suits first-time monthly renters, because a simple machine is easier to live with for thirty days than an exciting one, and the riding itself stays the pleasure rather than the machine becoming the project. Riders planning longer, faster or more carrying-heavy months may prefer the higher tier, and the fleet accommodates that choice at the published prices.</p>
<p>The deciding conversation is the same in every case: describe the month honestly at the counter, and let the verified fleet answer. More <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> details, including the full model lineup, are collected at the hub for exactly this comparison.</p>
<h2 id="sec-8">Availability and Next Steps</h2>
<p>The last honest fact is that availability is a today-question. Guides can quote verified prices; only the counter can quote today’s machines. For a monthly rental, arriving or calling early in the stay matters more than for a daily one, because a month is harder to improvise than a weekend.</p>
<p>The next steps are short: read the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a> for the full price structure, compare the Sirius against its tier-mates, then confirm the exact monthly figure and the machine at Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00.</p>
<p>A month of riding in Hanoi, on a machine whose price was known in advance and confirmed in writing, is one of the simplest pleasures the city offers. The Sirius has been carrying riders through exactly that month for years, and the price of joining them is the verified range this page began with.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions about the Yamaha Sirius monthly price</h2>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>How much does it cost to rent a Yamaha Sirius for a month in Hanoi?</h2>
<p>The verified monthly rate is 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND, and the exact figure for the machine you are offered depends on condition and availability, confirmed at the counter. For comparison, the daily rate is 150,000 VND and the weekly rate 700,000 VND, all verified figures from the shop at 112 Nguyen Van Cu.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>Why is the monthly price a range instead of one number?</h2>
<p>Because the fleet contains multiple machines whose condition and availability vary, and the shop quotes only figures it can stand behind. Plan against the top of the range and confirm the exact number at the counter before committing, which takes minutes and removes all guesswork.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>Is monthly rental cheaper than daily for the Sirius?</h2>
<p>In arithmetic terms, yes for most riding-heavy stays: the monthly range equals roughly six to eight days at the daily rate of 150,000 VND. Riders using the machine only a handful of days a month may still prefer daily or weekly terms, and the comparison can be finished on paper using the verified rates before deciding.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>How does the Sirius monthly price compare with other models?</h2>
<p>The Sirius shares its 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND monthly range with the Honda Wave, Honda Click and Yamaha Mio, forming the budget tier. The Honda Air Blade is a flat 1,500,000 VND per month and the Honda Vision 1,800,000 to 2,000,000 VND per month. The cost hub lays out every model side by side.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>What should I ask before renting a Sirius for a month?</h2>
<p>Ask where the available machines sit inside the monthly range, what the terms cover, the procedure if the machine needs attention during the month, and the exact figure written on your paperwork. The staff at 112 Nguyen Van Cu answer these questions daily, open 09:00 to 21:00.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>Can I reserve a Sirius for a monthly rental in advance?</h2>
<p>Availability is a same-day question, so the honest instruction is to contact the shop directly at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, or visit during opening hours, 09:00 to 21:00 daily. For a monthly rental, arranging it early in your stay matters more than for a daily one.</p>
